  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nTonight - Saturday: Benign Conditions and Above Normal \nTemperatures Continue\n\nAnomalously strong 500hPa ridging reflected throughout the \ntroposphere remains the dominant synoptic feature across United \nStates through the end of the week, slowly shifting eastward \nwith time. As such, rather benign conditions are expected \nthrough Saturday. \n\nOne exception to the benign conditions is overnight tonight \nwhen a favorable set up for valley fog develops across the \nregion. As the dominant ridge continues eastward, the 850hPa \nridge axis is expected to set up overhead tonight, resulting in \nclear skies and calm to light winds up to 4-5kft per 21.12z RRFS\nsoundings. Southerly winds and increasing dewpoints combined \nwith these quiescent conditions should lead to at least valley \nfog development overnight, though may ultimately become fairly \nwidespread with the 21.12z REFS suggesting 30-40% probabilities \nfor visibilities less than 1 mile outside of the valleys. Given \nthe late time of the year and favorable conditions for fog, \nwould expect any development to linger through mid to late \nThursday morning, dissipating between 10-11 AM CDT. \n\nAbove normal temperatures are also expected through the\nremainder of the week as subsidence associated with the high \nand consistent, albeit weak, warm air advection at 850hPa, above\nnormal temperatures are expected to remain across the region. \nTo account for the potential of overachieving temperatures given\nsaid subsidence and warm 850hPa temperatures, have continued \nwith the trend of leaning towards the 90th percentile of \nguidance for high temperatures. \n\nSaturday Night - Sunday: Precipitation Potential Returns\n\nUpper level ridging begins to break down and flatten late \nSaturday through Sunday as shortwave troughing across Canada \nmoves eastward. A surface cold front associated with this trough\nlays out somewhere across Minnesota into northern Wisconsin \nSaturday afternoon into Sunday, along the western and northern \nborders of our area. Southerly winds pool moisture along the \nboundary which should result in some low potential for \nprecipitation development given weak forcing along the front \ndespite the boundary and deeper moisture staying largely out of \nour area. Current probabilities for light rainfall sit around\n10-20% in the 21.12z NBM late Saturday into Sunday morning. If \nthis front were to sink further south, precipitation probabilities\nwould increase over a greater portion of our area.\n\nEarly Next Week: Trending Cooler and Wetter\n\nStill quite a bit of uncertainty regarding how early next week \nwill play out as many solutions are depicted amongst the various\nmodel suites as another shortwave moves through the region. \nWhile the LREF cluster analysis doesn't really hone in on a \nfavored scenario, all of the clusters do suggest some \nprobability for precipitation for Monday into Tuesday though the\nlocation and amounts of any rainfall are quite variable. Regardless,\nthere does appear to be some increasing confidence in precipitation\nMonday into Tuesday, around a 30-50% chance per the 21.13z NBM \nwhile the overall temperature trend within the ensemble \nguidance is towards seasonable temperatures.",
